- What failure modes require compensating actions to maintain exactly-once semantics?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to integrate policy-driven data masking with AWS DVA components, ensure regional data residency, and preserve auditability while maintaining low latency.

## Key Concepts

- Ingestion-time masking and policy evaluation
- EU data residency, per-tenant masking rules
- Policy engine integration (OPA) with Lambda/Fargate workflows
- Immutable audit trails (S3 Object Lock, DynamoDB logs)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskPII(record, fields) {
  const out = {...record};
  fields.forEach(f => {
    if (typeof out[f] === 'string') {
      const v = out[f];
      out[f] = v.replace(/.(?=.{4})/g, '*');
    }
  });
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate that EU masking rules are enforced without impacting non-EU tenants, and how would you test the policy at scale?
- What tracing and audit mechanisms would you implement to ensure data lineage and integrity after masking, including replay considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design content-based routing, with strict per-record validation and multi-stream fan-out. Emphasizes end-to-end tracing, fault handling, and test strategy in a realistic, beginner-friendly context.

## Key Concepts
- Content-based routing with multiple Kinesis streams
- Lambda validation of JSON schema and required fields
- Per-record tracing with AWS X-Ray
- Parquet writing to S3 and DLQ for invalid data
- LocalStack end-to-end testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-validator outline
function validate(record) {
  const hasFields = record.deviceId && record.ts && record.correlationId && typeof record.severity === 'number';
  const okSeverity = record.severity >= 0 && record.severity <= 100;
  return hasFields && okSeverity;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle backpressure if one stream lags significantly?
- What metrics would you emit to monitor data quality and latency?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical knowledge of runtime schema validation, per-tenant partitioning, and end-to-end observability in a beginner-friendly context. It focuses on safe schema evolution, reliable error handling, and basic testing with LocalStack.

## Key Concepts
- AWS Glue Schema Registry for runtime JSON schema management
- Lambda-based validation with quality scoring logic
- S3 Parquet partitioning strategy by tenant and date
- DLQ handling for invalid records using SQS
- X-Ray tracing across producer, validation, and write stages
- LocalStack testing strategy for pipeline validation

## Code Example
```javascript
const { GlueSchemaRegistryClient, RegisterSchemaCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-glue-schema-registry");
const AWSXRay = require("aws-xray-sdk");

AWSXRay.enableAutomaticSampling();
AWSXRay.captureHTTPsGlobal(require("http"));

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const segment = AWSXRay.getSegment();
  const subsegment = segment.addNewSubsegment("validation");

  try {
    const qualityScore = computeQualityScore(event);

    if (qualityScore < 0.5 || !validateSchema(event)) {
      await sendToDLQ(event, qualityScore);
      return { statusCode: 400, body: "Invalid record" };
    }

    await writeToS3Parquet(event, qualityScore);
    return { statusCode: 200, body: "Success" };
  } finally {
    subsegment.close();
  }
};

function computeQualityScore(record) {
  const requiredFields = ["deviceId", "tenantId", "timestamp", "payload"];
  const presentFields = requiredFields.filter(f => record[f] !== undefined);
  return presentFields.length / requiredFields.length;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests pragmatic security and governance for multi-tenant streaming workloads, ensuring key rotation and encryption strategies do not break ordering or inflight processing.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant CMEK and envelope encryption
- Tenant isolation via IAM, DynamoDB tenancy map
- Re-encryption workflows and retention

